<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Go to the Window. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Go to the Window.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow along during group story time.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'Go to the door.' or 'Show me the book.').
Can follow along during group story time, with gestures or with single-word responses (e.g., 'Where's the dog?' and the student responds, 'There.').
Can respond to simple questions by pointing or with single-word responses (e.g., 'Where is the pen?' and the student responds, 'There' or 'Where is the notebook? Is it on the desk or under the desk?' and the student responds, 'On').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to prepare students for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using classroom objects and simple movements. For example, "Simon says, point to the window!" (Theme: Go to the Window.), "Simon says, go to your chair!", "Simon says, touch your desk!", "Simon says, show me your book!" This activates listening and introduces key nouns and verbs. The teacher can introduce "Where is the book?" as they move around the classroom.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models clear pronunciation of new vocabulary (e.g., 'window,' 'chair,' 'desk,' 'book,' 'pencil,' 'bag') and prepositions ('on,' 'under'). The teacher uses TPR by physically demonstrating actions (e.g., putting a book *on* the desk, putting a pencil *under* the chair) while saying the words slowly and clearly. The teacher explicitly highlights the /w/ sound in 'where' and 'window' and the /b/ sound in 'book' through exaggerated pronunciation and repetition. The teacher also introduces simple phonemic awareness by showing flashcards with letters K, L, M, N, O and saying their sounds, connecting them to words if possible (e.g., K for key, M for marker). For polite requests, the teacher models "Show me the blue crayon, please" using a calm, friendly tone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple yes/no questions and requiring pointing or action.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a book *under* a table) "Is the book *on* the table?" (Students respond "No" or shake head). "Where is the book?" (Students point *under* the table).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a pencil) "Show me the pencil." (Students point to their own pencils or the teacher's pencil).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher says) "Go to the door." (Students perform the action). "Did you go to the window?" (Students respond "No" or shake head).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language and content in a meaningful context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book or digital storybook titled "Where is My Toy?" (narrative excerpt). The story features a child looking for their toys, using prepositions of place and showing various classroom/home objects like 'book', 'bag', 'chair', 'desk', 'box', 'table'. The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to the illustrations as they mention objects and locations. The story includes phrases like "The red ball is *under* the chair," "The blue book is *on* the table," "The yellow box is *next to* the desk." It will also include the command "Go to the window" as part of the search.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.'). Sociolinguistic: Giving information about locations of objects: Responding with basic location phrases (e.g., 'It's on the desk.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> While reading, the teacher pauses and asks students to listen and perform simple actions or point.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the child looking for?" (Students might say "toy" or point to a toy). "Where is the child going?" (Students point to locations in the book).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Pointing):</b> "Is the book *on* the chair?" (Teacher points to the book *under* the chair in the picture. Students point up for "true" or down for "false").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> "Show me the red ball." (Students point to the red ball in the picture). "Show me where the ball is. Is it *on* or *under* the chair?" (Students point to the correct preposition in the picture).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Controlled and semi-controlled practice to build accuracy in using the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening with a focus on Grammar: Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to, behind), WH-Questions for Location ('Where is the book? It is on the desk.'), Plural nouns in simple responses (e.g., 'three books'). Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil, key, marker, shelf, poster, ruler, glue, folder, card. Verbs: point, put, go, show. Colors: yellow, red, blue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Listen and Do":</b> The teacher gives clear, single-step instructions using target vocabulary and prepositions. Students listen and perform the action using realia or classroom objects. Examples: "Put the *red* pencil *in* the box." "Put the *large* book *on* the table." "Go *next to* the shelf." "Show me the *blue* folder." "Pick up *three* markers." The teacher constantly models correct pronunciation, especially for /w/ and /b/, and praises students for following instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Where is it?" Game:</b> The teacher places several familiar objects (e.g., book, pencil, bag) in various locations around the classroom. The teacher asks, "Where is the book?" Students listen and point to the book. The teacher then models the answer, "It's *on* the desk." Students repeat chorally. The teacher varies the questions: "Where is the pencil?", "Is the bag *under* the chair or *on* the chair?" (Students point and say "Under" or "On"). This reinforces listening for prepositions and WH-questions. The teacher also asks students to listen for numbers, e.g., "Where are the *three* books?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listening Focus):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives an instruction to the whole class, e.g., "Put your ruler *next to* your notebook." Students follow the instruction. Then, the teacher says, "Look at your partner's ruler. Is it *next to* the notebook?" Partner A points to their ruler, Partner B confirms by nodding or saying "Yes." This is a listening check by observation and simple confirmation rather than complex dialogue, suitable for Kinder.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task allowing students to use the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Treasure Hunt" (Listening-focused). The teacher hides several common classroom objects (e.g., a 'key', a 'small red box', a 'yellow marker', a 'blue card') in visible but specific locations around the classroom. The task directly connects to the scenario "Where Is It?" and theme "Go to the Window." The teacher gives verbal clues one at a time. Students listen carefully and follow the instructions to find the objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Let's find the treasures! Listen carefully to my words."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clue 1: "Go to the window. Look *under* the plant. Where is the *small red box*?" (Students listen, walk to the window, look under the plant, and find the box. They point to it or pick it up.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clue 2: "Go to the teacher's desk. The *yellow marker* is *in front of* the books. Where is the *yellow marker*?" (Students listen, go to the desk, and find the marker).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clue 3: "Look *behind* the chair, *next to* the table. The *blue card* is there. Where is the *blue card*?" (Students listen and find the card).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides encouragement and repeats clues if needed, emphasizing prepositions and object names. The focus is on the students' ability to listen and correctly interpret the location-based commands and questions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in following instructions, collaborative interaction (as a whole class or small groups), and successful completion of the communicative task (finding the hidden objects).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and self-reflection to check learning and progress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Treasure Hunt" and a final individual check. The teacher notes which students consistently follow instructions, correctly identify objects, and use pointing or single-word responses accurately.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives three new individual instructions, one for each student, or selects 3-4 students to perform tasks while others observe.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Go to the shelf. Show me the *yellow* book."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put the *large* marker *in* the box."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the *blue* folder? Is it *on* the chair or *under* the chair?" (Student points or says "On" or "Under").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can correctly identify objects upon listening to instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow 1-2 step instructions involving prepositions of place and classroom objects.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "Where is...?" by pointing or with single-word responses (e.g., 'There,' 'On,' 'Under').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows listening comprehension of words like 'large,' 'small,' and colors.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show their understanding by performing a non-verbal action or giving a simple response before leaving.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me with your thumb: Thumbs up if you listened carefully today! Thumbs down if it was hard."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you find the book *on* the table?" (Students point to the correct location or shake head).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunities for students and teacher to reflect on the learning experience.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear today? (e.g., 'book,' 'desk,' 'on,' 'under') (Teacher prompts with flashcards or objects to elicit single-word responses).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you go to the window?" (Students perform the action).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR was highly effective, visual cues from the storybook engaged students, the "Simon Says" warm-up set a good tone).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Need to increase the complexity of multi-step instructions slightly, provide more opportunities for individual pointing responses, perhaps a short song about prepositions for additional auditory input). Some students struggled with 'behind' vs 'in front of' – more direct TPR modeling is needed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Next time, we will look at a story about a classroom and you will *read* some of these words to find out where things are!" This sets the stage for Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension, using the same vocabulary. </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">2 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>